@*
    The one interactive island on the page (§3.11 C12). Everything around it is static SSR;
    the render mode is named where this component is *used*, never here and never at the root.

    It subscribes to the host's board — which is folded from a watcher's connection and from
    nothing else — and re-renders when the table says something. There is no second route to
    the session: what cannot reach this component cannot be drawn by it (P13.3 acceptance 2).

    🔥 A TABLE, NOT A DOCUMENT (BUILD-PLAN P13.5). The felt is a grid of named areas and every
    seat is placed in one by name; you are at the front of it whichever seat number you were
    dealt, and the others go clockwise from your left. The rotation is TableRing's — a pure
    function of the seating and of which seat is yours, unit-tested, and with no pixels in it.

    ⚠️ The seats are in TURN ORDER, CLOCKWISE FROM YOUR LEFT AND ENDING WITH YOURS — which is
    both the DOM order and the order they go round the felt, and the order they stack in when
    the felt is too narrow to be a ring. Nothing here uses `order:` or absolute placement, which
    is exactly how a ring of seats scrambles the tab order (§3.11 B6). There is no focusable
    control in a seat panel at all, so what this really protects is the order a screen reader
    reads the table in — and turn order is the one that means something at a card table.
*@
@implements IDisposable
@inject TableHost Host

<section class="table" aria-labelledby="table-heading">
    <header class="board">
        @*
            ⚠️ The page's one <h1>, and it lives here rather than on the page around it. The
            table used to be introduced by a heading that said "The table" above a heading that
            said which round it was, which is one heading too many for a page that is a table
            (BUILD-PLAN P13.5). Everything below it — your seat, the log, the settlement — is an
            <h2>, so the outline is still an outline.
        *@
        <h1 id="table-heading">
            @if (_board.Round == 0)
            {
                <text>Dealing…</text>
            }
            else
            {
                <text>Round @_board.Round</text>
            }
        </h1>

        @if (!_yours)
        {
            @* ⚠️ Said out loud: a watcher has no seat, so the front one is not theirs. *@
            <p class="watching">
                <span class="glyph" aria-hidden="true">👁</span>
                <span>Watching. No seat here is yours; the front one is the first dealt.</span>
            </p>
        }
    </header>

    <div class="felt seats-@_ring.Count" role="group" aria-label="The table">
        <TableCentre Board="_board" />

        @*
            ⚠️ role="list" is belt and braces. The list is display:contents so that its seats
            become items of the felt's own grid, and an implicit list role has historically been
            dropped when a list box disappears from layout; naming it keeps the semantics
            whatever the engine does.
        *@
        <ol class="ring" role="list" aria-label="The seats, in turn order, ending with yours">
            @foreach (var place in _ring)
            {
                <SeatPanel @key="place.Player.Value"
                           Seat="_board.SeatOf(place.Player)"
                           Seated="place.Place"
                           Yours="place.IsYours" />
            }
        </ol>
    </div>

    <YourSeat />

    <RoundLogPanel Lines="_board.Log" />

    @if (_board.Settlement is { } settled)
    {
        <SettlementPanel Result="settled" Seats="_seats" />
    }

    <div class="asides">
        <TableLegend />
        <AboutTable Seed="Host.Seed" />
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    private TableBoard _board = default!;
    private IReadOnlyList<SeatLine> _seats = [];
    private IReadOnlyList<RingPlace> _ring = [];
    private bool _yours;

    /// <remarks>
    /// ⚠️ <b>Runs twice</b> — once while the page is prerendered on the request, and again when
    /// the circuit starts (§3.11 C13). <see cref="TableHost.Start"/> is idempotent for exactly
    /// that reason: joining a table twice is a real bug, not a theoretical one.
    /// </remarks>
    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        Host.Start();
        Take();
        Host.Changed += OnTableSaidSomething;
    }

    /// <remarks>
    /// <b>The board arrives on the round's own thread</b>, which is not the renderer's, so the
    /// re-render goes through <c>InvokeAsync</c> (§3.11 C15). Nothing calls
    /// <c>StateHasChanged</c> from an event handler of its own — <c>ComponentBase</c> already
    /// re-renders after one.
    /// </remarks>
    private void OnTableSaidSomething()
    {
        Take();
        _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
    }

    /// <remarks>
    /// The ring is worked out here rather than per seat, so the whole table is laid out from one
    /// snapshot of the board and cannot be turned half way round mid-render.
    /// </remarks>
    private void Take()
    {
        _board = Host.Board;
        _seats = [.. _board.Seats];
        _ring = TableRing.Around(_board.Seating, Host.Seat);
        _yours = Host.Seat is not null;
    }

    /// <remarks>
    /// ⚠️ <b>Unhooking, and nothing else.</b> A circuit that dropped without this would keep a
    /// dead component subscribed to a live table (§3.11 A5) — and
    /// <c>StateHasChanged</c> must never be called from here, because disposal can run during
    /// renderer teardown where asking for a render is explicitly unsupported.
    /// </remarks>
    public void Dispose() => Host.Changed -= OnTableSaidSomething;
}
